// ============================================================================
//  momentary_switches.rules -- action switches release themselves
// ============================================================================
//  GENERATED by make_generated_rules.py -- edit that, not this file.
//  Variant: OH5 dev instance
//
//  THE PROBLEM
//  -----------
//  Every action switch in this system latches.  The rules fire on `changed` and
//  act only when the state is ON, and every auto-off timer was commented out:
//
//      // tnl_1_resetTimer_sw = createTimer(now.plusSeconds(5), [ | ... ])
//
//  So an operator flips a switch, the action runs, and the switch stays ON.  To
//  do it a second time they must first flip it OFF and then ON again -- and
//  pressing an already-ON switch does nothing at all, silently.  The operator
//  handbook carries a red warning box about this, which is a documentation fix
//  for a software problem.
//
//  THE FIX, IN TWO HALVES
//  ----------------------
//  Here: 2 s after the switch goes ON, it is put back to OFF.
//  On the sitemaps: `labelcolor=[<item>=="ON"="orange"]`, so the line turns
//  orange for those 2 s and then goes back.  The operator sees the action
//  register, and the control is immediately ready to be used again.
//
//  postUpdate, not sendCommand: the action rules trigger on `changed` and act
//  only on ON, so the release can never re-fire any of them.
//
//  NOT INCLUDED, DELIBERATELY: tnl_N_btnctrl_enable.  That switch is not an
//  action, it is the tunnel's ON/OFF STATE -- it is meant to stay where the
//  operator put it, and auto-releasing it would switch the plant off.
//
//  WHY ONE RULE PER SWITCH, AND NOT ONE RULE WITH 44 TRIGGERS
//  ---------------------------------------------------------
//  The obvious form is a single rule with all 44 triggers `or`-ed together,
//  using the implicit `triggeringItem` to know which one fired.  That was tried
//  first on OH5 and it does NOT work here: the rule registers, reports IDLE,
//  and silently never releases anything -- no exception, nothing in the log.
//  Naming each item explicitly relies on no implicit variable at all, and this
//  file is generated, so the repetition costs nothing to write or maintain.
// ============================================================================

rule "Release seasonStart"
when
    Item seasonStart received command ON
then
    createTimer(now.plusSeconds(2), [|
        seasonStart.postUpdate(OFF)
        logInfo("momentary", "seasonStart released")
    ])
end

